s is conditional on the theta-core topology. A bouquet core and differing definitions of edge-simple loops need separate treatment. The constraint alone supplies no weights for the limiting winding distribution and does not turn an arbitrary chosen cycle basis into the requested loop triple.\n\nSource: P0133-A001 and Schramm Problem 6.2 in the cached 2006 paper. Exact finite-grid enumeration will separately test the homology-basis determinant without asserting a scaling limit.\n\n## A bouquet witness under the literal no-repeated-edge convention\n\nFor every square torus grid of side L>=3, take the primal comb tree: in every horizontal row retain the L-1 nonwrapping horizontal edges, and in column zero retain the L-1 nonwrapping vertical edges. This is connected with L^2-1 edges and is a spanning tree.\n\nIts dual complement has, after pruning leaves, one horizontal loop and one vertical loop meeting at a single dual vertex. To check this directly, the omitted horizontal wrap edges give the full vertical dual loop in column L-1. The omitted vertical edges give horizontal dual chains in every row, with a wrap edge only in row L-1. All chains in the other rows prune away; row L-1 supplies the second loop.\n\nThese two loops have disjoint edge sets and windings (1,0) and (0,1), after choosing orientations. Traversing either alone, or concatenating the first with the second in either orientation, gives four noncontractible closed trails with no repeated edges. Up to reversing the complete trail, their distinct homotopy windings are\n\n    (1,0), (0,1), (1,1), (1,-1).\n\nThe concatenations repeat the common vertex but no edge. Thus, under the literal edge-simple definition given as 'no repeating edges' in the cached source, at least four classes occur for this finite-grid tree. A vertex-simple-cycle convention would give only the two individual loops. This requires reconciliation with the source's assertion of precisely three paths before turning it into a universal finite-grid statement. It is not a conclusion about the intended limiting distribution. The witness exists at arbitrarily fine grid sizes, but its probability may tend to zero.\n\nSource quote for audit: Schramm cached text, lines 1048-1049: 'contains precisely three edge-simple closed paths (i.e., no repeating edges)'. Ask the Claude reviewer to resolve the source convention; the new lemma itself only claims the four explicit trails under its stated convention.\n",
